Conjecture: open:creib.forge.conformance.records.recover_json_object gives the same answer for two replies that its documented rule requires to be different. The rule says: 'The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text'. Reply A is:
```json
{"a":1} {"b":2}
```
{"outside": 3}
Reply B is:
```json
{"a":1} {"c":4}
```
{"outside": 3}
For Reply A the last object inside the fence is {"b":2}, so the rule requires recover_json_object to return {"b":2}. For Reply B the last object inside the fence is {"c":4}, so the rule requires {"c":4}. But the function treats the whole fenced content as one candidate; since '{"a":1} {"b":2}' is not a single JSON object, that candidate is passed over, and the function falls back to the last top-level object, which is {"outside":3} in both replies. Thus it returns {"outside":3} for both, the same answer where the rule requires different answers. I would be wrong if recover_json_object(Reply A) returned {"b":2} and recover_json_object(Reply B) returned {"c":4}, or if the fenced content were parsed as a single object, or if top-level extraction excluded fenced content.